  (text "Commander keyboard - the microcontroller circuit, soldered straight onto the keyboard PCB in place of the Arduino\nMicro module (U1 of the Altium design).\n\nSince 2026-11-15 (cost target): STC8H8K64U-45I-LQFP32 (LCSC C5210757, about 0.35 USD in volume) instead of the\nATmega32U4 (about 3.30 USD).  It runs from the same 5 V, so the 84 infra-red LEDs and phototransistors of the matrix\nsee the same levels as on the prototype.  It needs no crystal (USB clock recovered from the bus), no reset circuit\nand no ISP pads: firmware goes in over the USB-C itself, through STC's USB boot loader, entered by holding the BOOT\nbutton (P3.2 to ground) while the cable is plugged in.  THE FIRMWARE IS NEW: the Arduino sketch of the prototype\ndoes not run on this 8051-core chip; the pin table below is what it has to read and drive (see the README).\n\nKept: USB-C (device side, 5.1 k on both CC pins), the ESD array, the 22 ohm series resistors, the resettable fuse\n(750 mA: the 84 infra-red LEDs draw about 0.56 A when all are on), the decoupling.\nNet names are still the ARDUINO pin names (D0..D13, A0..A5, MISO / SCK / MOSI) the prototype used: they name the\nmatrix lines, not chip pins any more.  References are in a 200-block (no collision with the Altium parts)." (exclude_from_sim no) (at 15.24 25.4 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.524 1.524)) (justify left top)) (uuid "68d19a95-0ac6-5e21-bc30-79127dcede06"))
